brigada init: set up example.invalid/imported

brigada runs coding tasks on the agent CLIs you are signed in to. A foreman
(an agent) turns your request into a plan of tasks; workers (other agents)
write the code in their own git worktrees; your check command tests it; and
an agent of a different model family reviews it. Three short steps follow.
Nothing is written or started until you confirm at the end.

Step 1 of 3: agents
  Each agent CLI below can work for brigada. For each one, pick the model it
  should run, or leave it out. Reviews always go to a different model family
  than the author's, so you need at least two families (e.g. claude + codex).

  claude: signed in
    Which model should claude run?
       1  sonnet               Anthropic  Sonnet 4 · Balanced
       2  opus                 Anthropic  Opus 4 · Most capable
       3  haiku                Anthropic  fastest; fine for small tasks
       4  claude-sonnet-4[1m]  Anthropic  Sonnet, 1M context
       5  claude-opus-4        Anthropic  Opus 4 · Most capable
       -  don't use claude
    Pick 1-5 or -, or type a model id [1]:
    How hard should sonnet think? More effort gives better results on hard
    tasks and uses more of your plan's limits.
       1  default  claude's default
       2  low
       3  medium
       4  high
       5  xhigh
       6  max
    Pick 1-6 [1]:

  codex: signed in
    Which model should codex run?
       1  gpt-6.1-sol   OpenAI  your codex default (~/.codex/config.toml);
                                General-purpose coding model.
       2  gpt-5         OpenAI  Earlier coding model.
       3  gpt-5.4-mini  OpenAI  A smaller coding model for quick tasks, with a
                                long description that must wrap within the menu
                                without losing any of its words.
       -  don't use codex
    1 more from `codex debug models` aren't listed: brigada can't tell their
    model family, which it needs to pick reviewers.
    Pick 1-3 or -, or type a model id [1]:
    How hard should gpt-6.1-sol think? More effort gives better results on hard
    tasks and uses more of your plan's limits.
       1  low     codex's default for this model
       2  medium
       3  high
       4  xhigh
       5  max
       6  ultra
    Pick 1-6 [1]:

  kimi: init can't check sign-in. If you're not signed in, run `kimi login`
  first, or leave kimi out.
    Use kimi? [y/N]:
    Which model should kimi run?
       1  kimi-code/k3                         Moonshot  your kimi default
       2  kimi-code/k3-256k                    Moonshot  K3, 256K context
       3  kimi-code/kimi-for-coding            Moonshot  200K context
       4  kimi-code/kimi-for-coding-highspeed  Moonshot  200K context
    Pick 1-4, or type a model id [1]:
    Effort: high, kimi's own setting for this model (change it in
    ~/.kimi-code/config.toml; brigada can't set it per run).
    kimi has no sandbox: when it writes code it can run any command on this
    machine without asking. As a reviewer it only reads.
    Let kimi write code too, not only review? [y/N]:

  opencode: signed in to 2 providers (opencode providers list)
    Which model should opencode run?
       1  zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3  Zhipu (GLM)  GLM-5.3, 200K context
       2  zai-coding-plan/glm-5.2  Zhipu (GLM)  GLM-5.2, 200K context
       3  opencode/deepseek-v3     DeepSeek     DeepSeek V3, 1M context
       -  don't use opencode
    Pick 1-3 or -, or type provider/model [-]:
    How hard should zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3 think? More effort gives better
    results on hard tasks and uses more of your plan's limits.
       1  default  opencode's default
       2  low
       3  medium
       4  high
    Pick 1-4 [1]:

Step 2 of 3: the foreman
  The foreman plans the work and decides what happens when a task needs
  judgment. It runs whenever work finishes, so it uses that agent's quota.
  Only claude and codex can be the foreman; it uses the model picked above
  and its effort.
    1  claude/sonnet
    2  codex/gpt-6.1-sol
  Pick 1-2 [1]:

Step 3 of 3: the check
  A command that must succeed (exit 0) before a task's work is accepted.
  Found uv.lock, so the suggestion is:
  Check command [uv run pytest]:

Plan
  Agents:
    claude    sonnet (default)                   Anthropic    writes code,
                                                              reviews
    codex     gpt-6.1-sol (default)              OpenAI       writes code,
                                                              reviews
    kimi      kimi-code/k3 (default)             Moonshot     reviews only
    opencode  zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3 (default)  Zhipu (GLM)  writes code,
                                                              reviews
  Reviews: 4 model families, so every task can get a reviewer of another one.
  Foreman: claude/sonnet
  Check: uv run pytest (init doesn't run it; change it later in
    .brigada/project.yaml)
  What workers may do:
    claude    edit files; run only: uv run pytest
    codex     edit files and run commands inside codex's sandbox
    opencode  edit files; no commands
  Files in this repository (brigada never commits them):
    .brigada/project.yaml                    new  the settings above
    .mcp.json                                new  lets Claude Code here use
                                                  brigada (Claude Code asks you
                                                  to approve it)
    .claude/settings.local.json              new  private, kept out of git:
                                                  when your Claude turn ends,
                                                  brigada's foreman picks up
                                                  what happened meanwhile
    .claude/skills/brigada-foreman/SKILL.md  new  rules for your Claude session
    (show full contents: --verbose)
  Codex, optional: to plan from your own Codex session, run once yourself:
    codex mcp add brigada-foreman -- brigada mcp --foreman
  In the background: a runner starts now and keeps going (log:
    ~/.local/state/brigada/runner.log; stop it with: brigada stop). The engine
    starts when needed and stops after 30 idle minutes.

To change later: brigada init (agents, models, effort),
  brigada project foreman (foreman), .brigada/project.yaml (check),
  brigada config (see everything).

Write 4 files and start the runner? [y/N]
Declined; nothing written or started.
